  "341561": {
    "id": 341561,
    "literal_statement": "Let $\\Sigma$ be an ordered alphabet. For words $u,v\\in\\Sigma^*$ and a letter $a\\in\\Sigma$, a word is **$M$-unambiguous** if it is not $M$-equivalent to any distinct word, where $M$-equivalence means having the same Parikh matrix. **Serbanuta's conjecture.** If $uaav$ is $M$-unambiguous, then $uav$ is $M$-unambiguous. Equivalently, if $uav$ is $M$-ambiguous, then $uaav$ is also $M$-ambiguous. This conjecture concerns the possible patterns of $M$-ambiguity under insertion of a repeated letter and was proposed as an open problem by Serbanuta; its resolution is not specified in the supplied text.",
