    "title": "Strong palindromic subsequence conjecture for binary circular words",
    "literal_statement": "Let $w$ be a **binary circular word** of length $n$ divisible by $6$. Choose a linear representation and partition it as $w=w_1w_2$ into two linear words of equal length. Let $s_1$ and $s_2$ be subsequences of $w_1$ and $w_2$, respectively, and let $s_2^R$ denote reversal.\n\n**Strong circular-palindrome conjecture.** There is such a partition with subsequences $s_1,s_2$ satisfying\n\n$$\ns_1=s_2^R\n$$\n\n\u2014that is, $s_1s_2$ is a palindrome\u2014and\n\n$$\n|s_1s_2|=\\frac{2}{3}|w|.\n$$\n\nThis strengthens the weak circular-palindrome conjecture by aligning the two halves of the palindrome with a cut into equal halves of the circle. The source notes that the bound $\\frac12n$ is proved, while no stronger bound is known.",
