    "title": "The simple-matrix conjecture on linear homogeneous submatrices",
    "literal_statement": "Let $P$) be a **simple** zero-one matrix if both $P$ and its complement $P^c$ are acyclic, meaning that every submatrix has a row or column containing at most one 1-entry. A matrix is **$P$-free** if it contains no submatrix equal to $P$. A submatrix is **homogeneous** if all its entries are equal.\n\n**Simple-matrix conjecture.** Let $P$ be a simple zero-one matrix. Then every $P$-free $n\\times n$ zero-one matrix contains a $cn\\times cn$ homogeneous submatrix, for a suitable constant $c>0$.\n\nThe preceding results establish this type of conclusion for the relevant $2\\times k$ patterns, while nonsimple matrices admit counterexamples with only sublinear homogeneous submatrices. The conjecture concerns the remaining simple patterns, including the finite cases not covered by the proved results.",
